d 'SMAIHDA': module to compute simple-means MAIHDA for binary outcomes
d
d  smaihda implements the simple-means form of the Multilevel Analysis
d  of Individual Heterogeneity and Discriminatory Accuracy (MAIHDA) for
d  binary outcomes, in two designs: individuals nested in contexts
d  (two-level), or individuals nested in cross-classified
d  sociodemographic-by-area strata nested in geographical areas
d  (three-level). All measures are derived from observed counts
d  and proportions, without random-effects modelling. The command
d  computes Wilson 95% CIs for proportions at three levels, EAR-
d  standardised area proportions, ARD/RR comparisons, stratum
d  deviations Delta_A,SD, the Contextual Clustering of
d  Inequalities (CCI, crude and standardised), variance partition
d  coefficients (VPC) on the probability scale, crude and
d  standardised (the latter representing the general contextual
d  effect independent of composition, uncorrected and
d  noise-corrected), discriminatory
d  accuracy (AUC, the size-standardised AUC in which every cell
d  counts once, sensitivity, specificity), and 95% confidence
d  intervals via a within-context bootstrap that holds the contexts
d  fixed and resamples individuals within each cell. The data can be
d  individual records or a stratum table with the number of individuals
d  and the number of cases of each stratum (option n()).
d
d KW: MAIHDA
d KW: simple means
d KW: variance partition coefficient
d KW: VPC
d KW: discriminatory accuracy
d KW: standardised AUC
d KW: health inequalities
d KW: within-context bootstrap
d KW: small area analysis
d
d Requires: Stata version 14
d
d Version: 1.9.0
d Companion to: Merlo J et al. "Describing health inequalities without
d distortion: Simple-Means MAIHDA vs Random-Effects MAIHDA." Preprint V.2.
d Distribution-Date: 20260926
d
d Author: Juan Merlo, Unit for Social Epidemiology, Lund University, Sweden
d Support: email juan.merlo@@med.lu.se
d
F smaihda.ado
